{% lede %}
At Origin, a 3D printing startup of about twenty people, I designed a company flag and pitched it in a 30-minute, 136-slide talk on how flags work. The team adopted it: it hung in the office, went onto swag and came along on outings.
{% /lede %}

## Good flag, bad flag

The idea came from Roman Mars's TED talk, [Why city flags may be the worst-designed thing you've never noticed](https://www.ted.com/talks/roman_mars_why_city_flags_may_be_the_worst_designed_thing_you_ve_never_noticed), and the design rules from the North American Vexillological Association's guide, [Good Flag, Bad Flag](https://nava.org/good-flag-bad-flag).

The talk started with the basics of vexillology: keep it simple, use meaningful symbolism, stick to two or three colors, skip lettering and seals, and be distinctive. Then the room played Good Flag, Bad Flag with real city and state flags. One tell of a good flag: people get it tattooed.

## The design

The flag uses Origin's three brand colors, each standing for a part of the business: black for hardware, white for software, blue for materials. The ring is the "O" of Origin. A construction sheet fixes every proportion, and a set of variations swaps the emblem for each team or recolors the field for holidays without losing the flag.
